<section id="how"><h2>How it works</h2>
<ul>
<li>Each thread picks a random start inside the puzzle range and scans consecutive private keys.</li>
<li>For every key it computes the public key (secp256k1), then SHA-256 and RIPEMD-160, and compares the result to the target hash160.</li>
<li>If it matches, the solver stops and shows the key, WIF, public key and address.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2>
<details><summary>Can this actually find the key?</summary><p>The range has about 1.2 &times; 10&sup2;&sup1; keys. A browser does thousands to tens of thousands per second per thread, so realistically no. The ETA above shows your own measured number.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens if a key is found?</summary><p>A result panel opens with the private key, WIF, public key and address, plus a verification badge.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is my data sent anywhere?</summary><p>No. All computation runs locally in Web Workers.</p></details>
</section>
<footer>Educational tool. Always run the Self-test first.</footer>
</div>

<div id="ov"><div class="box">
 <h2 style="margin-top:0">&#127881; Key found</h2>
